WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.520
- [Narrator] Okay, so here's a hot take.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:04.920
Pandas are overrated.

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.870
Sure, they're cute. So cute that

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:10.390
they show up in our logos, our
movies, and even on our feet.

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:11.730
We are obsessed.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:14.430
But every obsession has a dark side.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:16.363
And this one starts in China.

00:00:16.363 --> 00:00:19.863
(bubbly orchestral music)

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.100
The Chinese government owns nearly

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.210
all the giant pandas on earth.

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.220
And American zoos will shell out up to

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:33.600
a million dollars a year to rent just one.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.790
Most sign 10 year panda
diplomacy contracts,

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:38.460
and if any baby cubs are born,

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:43.340
they pay an additional
one-time $400,000 baby tax.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.300
That doesn't even include the extra costs,

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.420
like the bear's enclosure, medical care,

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.330
and mountains of bamboo, but zoos are far

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:52.650
from going bankrupt over pandas.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.240
In 2012, attendance at the Edinburgh Zoo

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:58.650
rose 51% after pandas arrived.

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:02.270
And the National and Atlanta
zoos saw similar increases.

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:04.240
While that money is great for the zoos,

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:06.883
it's not necessarily
the best for the pandas.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.960
Turns out, breeding pandas in captivity

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.360
is a completely different
ball game from in the wild.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:28.960
Threesomes are more or less the norm,

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.130
when it comes to wild panda sex,

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:34.780
something that's really
difficult to replicate in zoos.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:37.460
On top of that, wild pandas
have a mating routine

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:39.330
that involves courting calls and scent

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:42.600
markings that can last
weeks, but in captivity,

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:45.290
the chosen pair is often
separated, right up to when

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:47.980
the female is ovulating, and as a result,

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:49.690
they rarely hit it off.

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:53.500
Regardless, we are dead set
on keeping the species alive.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:54.333
And why wouldn't we?

00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:56.130
They're freaking adorable.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.340
The round heads, seemingly large eyes,

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:01.650
and Cabbage Patch Kid
bodies remind us of babies.

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:04.080
And that triggers a nurturing response.

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:07.330
Ultimately, what it boils down to is this:

00:02:07.330 --> 00:02:09.670
People care about cute critters.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:10.503
Period.

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:25.790
Mangrove forests, for
example, support at least

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:29.820
1,300 animal species, with their
roots, fruit, and branches.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:33.350
So, saving them is like
saving their entire ecosystem.

00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:37.110
Pandas, by comparison, don't
affect many other life forms.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:40.870
So, saving them saves the
pandas, but not much else.

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:44.170
Yet, it's the cuddly pandas
who qualify as a poster species

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:47.730
for conservation organizations,
not the mangroves.

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:49.240
And that's a problem.

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:52.037
Of the close to 20,000
endangered animal species,

00:02:52.037 --> 00:02:56.460
only 80 get the lion's share
of the already scarce funding.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:59.500
And one study found
that 61% of conservation

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.850
campaigns only raised funds
for these celebrity species.

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:06.300
But as the World Wildlife Fund's logo,

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:09.950
pandas really hit the
ultimate conservation jackpot.

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:12.900
When the World Wildlife
Fund was founded in 1961,

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:16.740
it distributed all printed
materials in black and white.

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:19.370
You know what else was black and white?

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:20.690
Pandas.

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:22.700
Pandas look just as cute in color

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:26.150
as they did in grayscale, so voila.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:29.010
But the sad irony is
the main reason pandas

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:32.400
were endangered in the first
place is habitat destruction.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:34.010
Yet, even with all their funding,

00:03:34.010 --> 00:03:37.030
it's not enough to prevent
further habitat decline.

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.960
Construction, logging,
and an influx of tourists

00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:59.950
continued to diminish and disjoint

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:01.840
the panda's natural habitat.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.100
And if the whole point
of these hefty funds

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:05.810
and breeding programs is to rebuild

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:08.750
wild populations, we're sucking at it.

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:11.260
In 2006, the Chinese government started

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:13.270
reintroducing pandas to the wild,

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:16.258
but only five of those
seven are still alive.

00:04:16.258 --> 00:04:19.410
In fact, of all the species
listed as endangered

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:23.170
since 1973, 99% are still around,

00:04:23.170 --> 00:04:27.090
but only 1%, one, have been successfully

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.230
rehabilitated and taken off the list.

00:04:47.170 --> 00:04:50.470
Maybe it's time to end the
era of pent up panda bears.

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:53.090
After all, we'll always
have Kung Fu Panda.

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:56.423
(soft electronic music)

